1. Define the Problem You Exist to Solve
Before forming anything, get precise about the real-world problem.

Identify the population or community you intend to serve.

Describe the problem in plain language.

Identify what is already being done by other nonprofits, agencies, businesses, schools, or faith organizations.

Determine whether there is a genuine unmet need—or whether partnership with an existing organization would create more impact than starting a new one.

Gather evidence:

Community interviews

Surveys

Public data

Local reports

Conversations with service providers

Input from the people you intend to serve

Define what “success” looks like for beneficiaries, not just for the organization.

Deliverable: a short needs statement explaining who needs help, what they need, why current options are insufficient, and how your organization will help.

2. Choose Your Mission, Vision, and Theory of Change
Your mission should be narrow enough to guide decisions but broad enough to last.

Write a mission statement:

Who you serve

What you do

Intended result

Write a vision statement:

The long-term change you want to see in the world or community

Define your values:

Accountability

Respect

Inclusion

Stewardship

Transparency

Safety

Other principles relevant to your cause

Build a simple theory of change:

Inputs: money, volunteers, staff, partnerships

Activities: programs and services

Outputs: direct work completed

Outcomes: measurable change for people or communities

Impact: long-term result

Deliverable: a one-page mission-and-impact model.

3. Decide Whether You Need a New Nonprofit
A new nonprofit is not always the best starting point.

Consider three paths:

Start an independent nonprofit

Best when your mission, governance, funding plan, and programs are truly distinct.

Use a fiscal sponsor

An established nonprofit may accept donations and administer funds for your project while you develop capacity.

Often useful for testing a concept before creating a separate organization.

Partner with an existing nonprofit

Best if a credible organization already serves the same population and can adopt or host your program.

Starting independently means taking on ongoing governance, bookkeeping, reporting, fundraising, insurance, compliance, and operational responsibilities—not just launching a cause.

Deliverable: a written decision explaining why an independent entity, fiscal sponsorship, or partnership is the best path.

4. Create a Basic Organizational Model
Decide how the organization will actually operate.

Define your initial programs or services.

Decide whether services will be:

Direct services

Grants to others

Education and outreach

Advocacy within applicable legal limits

Events

Research

Volunteer-led projects

Digital resources

Define your service area:

Neighborhood

City

State

National

International

Identify key partners:

Schools

Community organizations

Local government

Healthcare providers

Businesses

Foundations

Faith communities

Identify what you will not do during the first year.

Deliverable: a first-year operating plan with a limited number of programs.

5. Build a Founding Board of Directors
A nonprofit needs governance, not just a founder.

Build a board with people who collectively bring:

Commitment to the mission

Financial literacy

Legal or compliance awareness

Fundraising ability

Community credibility

Program expertise

Technology or communications skills

Lived experience relevant to the people served

Independence and willingness to provide oversight

Early board responsibilities should include:

Approving the mission and strategic direction

Approving the budget

Adopting policies

Overseeing the executive leader or founder

Reviewing financial information

Supporting fundraising

Managing conflicts of interest

Protecting the organization’s reputation and mission

Avoid creating a board made up only of friends, relatives, or people unwilling to challenge decisions.

Deliverable: a board roster, role descriptions, meeting schedule, and board recruitment plan.

6. Establish Legal Structure and State Formation
For many U.S. charitable organizations, the common route is to form a nonprofit corporation in the state where the organization will operate. State law controls formation requirements, corporate filings, registered-agent requirements, and many charitable-solicitation rules; those requirements are not identical everywhere. Federal tax exemption is a separate process. 
