At a high level, this stage usually includes:

Choose an organization name.

Confirm the name is available under applicable state rules.

Select a registered agent if required.

Prepare and file formation documents, often called articles of incorporation.

Ensure the formation documents contain language appropriate for your intended tax-exempt purpose.

Obtain an Employer Identification Number (EIN).

Create a formal organizational records file.

Hold an organizational board meeting.

Adopt bylaws.

Approve initial officers.

Approve essential policies.

Create board minutes documenting those actions.

Do not assume that incorporation alone makes donations tax-deductible or creates federal tax-exempt status.

Deliverable: legally formed entity, EIN, bylaws, organizational resolutions, and a secure records repository.

7. Seek Federal Tax-Exempt Status, If Appropriate
Many public charities seek recognition under Internal Revenue Code section 501(c)(3), but that status is not automatic. The IRS provides different application pathways and forms depending on the organization type and eligibility; Form 1023 is the principal application for recognition of 501(c)(3) status, while Form 1023-EZ may be available for certain eligible smaller organizations. 
