At this stage:

Confirm the type of tax-exempt organization you intend to be.

Determine whether public charity status, private foundation status, or another structure applies.

Prepare organizational and financial information.

Develop a realistic narrative of activities.

Prepare projected budgets.

Identify compensation plans, if any.

Disclose conflicts, related-party relationships, and governance practices accurately.

Submit the appropriate IRS application when ready.

Keep copies of all filings and determination letters.

Get qualified legal or tax advice before filing if your activities involve complex fundraising, political activity, international work, scholarships, private benefits, substantial business activity, or regulated services.

Deliverable: a complete exemption application package and, if approved, an IRS determination letter.

8. Register for State and Local Compliance Requirements
Your obligations may continue beyond formation and federal tax exemption.

Potential requirements can include:

State tax-exemption applications

Charitable-solicitation registration before asking the public for donations

Annual corporate reports

State financial reports

Local business licenses

Sales-tax exemptions

Payroll registrations

Employment-related registrations

Permits for events, food service, facilities, childcare, transportation, or other regulated activities

If you fundraise online, social media, email, or through a website, you may reach donors in multiple states. Plan for a compliance review before broad public solicitation.

Deliverable: a compliance calendar listing every filing, renewal, responsible person, fee, and deadline.

9. Create Governance Policies Before Problems Occur
Policies protect the mission, board, staff, volunteers, beneficiaries, and donors.

Start with:

Conflict-of-interest policy

Whistleblower or reporting-concerns policy

Document retention and destruction policy

Financial controls policy

Expense-reimbursement policy

Gift acceptance policy

Donor privacy policy

Volunteer policy

Code of conduct

Anti-harassment policy

Safeguarding policy, especially if serving children or vulnerable populations

Data privacy and cybersecurity policy

Social media policy

Records-management policy

Crisis communications policy

Deliverable: a board-approved policy manual that is reviewed at least annually.

10. Build the Financial Foundation
Good accounting is mission protection. You need to know where money came from, what restrictions apply, where it went, and whether it advanced the mission.

Set up:

A nonprofit bank account separate from personal accounts.

Accounting software or a qualified bookkeeping process.

A chart of accounts designed for nonprofit reporting.

A budget for the first year.

Cash-flow forecasts.

Donation and grant tracking.

Restricted-fund tracking.

Expense approval rules.

Two-person controls for payments where practical.

Monthly bank reconciliations.

Monthly financial reports for the board.

A process for receipts, invoices, contracts, and reimbursements.

An annual budget approval process.

A reserve policy as the organization becomes stable.

Never mix personal and organizational money.

Deliverable: approved budget, accounting system, financial-control checklist, and monthly reporting package.

11. Plan Tax, Reporting, and Donor Receipts
Most tax-exempt organizations must file an annual information return or notice with the IRS. Failure to file required Form 990-series returns or notices for three consecutive years generally results in automatic revocation of federal tax-exempt status. Form 990 filings also report activities, finances, governance, and compliance information, and much of the filing is publicly available. 
