Deliverable: a recurring annual compliance calendar and standardized donation-receipt process.

12. Develop a Sustainable Funding Strategy
Do not depend on one fundraiser, one grant, or one generous person.

Build a balanced revenue strategy that may include:

Individual donors

Monthly donors

Major gifts

Foundation grants

Corporate sponsorships

Corporate matching gifts

Events

Peer-to-peer fundraising

Program fees, where appropriate

Government grants or contracts

In-kind donations

Earned revenue that is mission-aligned

Planned giving later in the organization’s life cycle

For each funding source, define:

Who is responsible

Expected revenue

Cost to raise the funds

Timing

Restrictions

Reporting burden

Renewal likelihood

Risk if the funding disappears

Be transparent about how online donations are processed, whether a platform or intermediary is involved, whether fees apply, and who provides the donor receipt. The Federal Trade Commission notes that online charitable-giving portals should make clear how donated funds move and whether fees are retained. 
ftc.gov

Deliverable: a 12-month fundraising plan and donor stewardship process.

13. Design Programs That Can Be Measured
A nonprofit should be able to answer: “What changed because of our work?”

For every program:

Define the target group.

Define eligibility criteria.

Define services provided.

Define expected outputs.

Define short-, medium-, and long-term outcomes.

Select a small number of meaningful metrics.

Determine how data will be collected ethically.

Decide who reviews the data and how often.

Use feedback from beneficiaries to improve the program.

Document lessons learned.

Do not collect personal information just because it might be useful later. Collect only the data needed for service delivery, evaluation, safety, legal compliance, and reporting.

Deliverable: a program logic model and simple measurement plan.

14. Build Your Brand and Public Identity
Your brand should make it easy for people to understand and trust you.

Create:

Organization name

Logo and visual identity

Mission tagline

Plain-language explanation of your work

Brand voice and writing style

Core messages for donors, volunteers, beneficiaries, partners, and media

Founder story, used carefully and truthfully

Impact stories that protect participant privacy and dignity

Photo and media-consent practices

Public FAQ

Your message should answer:

Who do you help?

What do you do?

Why does it matter?

Why should someone trust you?

How can someone donate, volunteer, partner, or receive services?

Deliverable: a basic brand guide and messaging document.

15. Build the Website and Digital Foundation
Your website should be an operational asset—not just an online brochure.

Your first website should eventually include:

Home page

About page

Mission, vision, and values

Programs or services pages

“Get help” or service-access page, if applicable

Donate page

Volunteer page

Partner or sponsor page

Contact page

Leadership and board page

Financial transparency page

News, updates, or impact stories

Privacy policy

Terms or website-use notice as appropriate

Accessibility considerations

Donation receipts and confirmation process

Secure contact forms

Email newsletter sign-up

Analytics configured with privacy in mind

For your custom-built site, plan the architecture before coding:

Domain-name strategy

Hosting provider

Content-management approach

Front-end framework or plain HTML/CSS/JavaScript decision

Back-end language and database

Authentication requirements

Donation processor integration

Email platform

CRM or donor-management integration

Backups

Logging

Security monitoring

Update and maintenance process

Ownership of source code, accounts, credentials, and vendor contracts

At minimum, use HTTPS, strong unique passwords, multi-factor authentication where available, timely software updates, backups, and vulnerability management. CISA specifically recommends HTTPS with HSTS for external-facing sites and emphasizes remediating critical and high vulnerabilities. 
