The Most Common Early Mistakes to Avoid
Starting without validating the need.

Having a mission that is too broad.

Treating the board as a formality.

Mixing personal and nonprofit funds.

Waiting too long to set up bookkeeping.

Assuming incorporation equals tax exemption.

Fundraising publicly before checking registration requirements.

Collecting personal data without a clear need or protection plan.

Building an expensive website before defining programs and audiences.

Using personal accounts to control nonprofit domains, bank access, social media, or donation platforms.

Relying on one donor, one grant, or one founder.

Measuring activity but not outcomes.

Overstating impact.

Failing to file annual reports or required IRS returns. Required federal annual filings matter: missing them for three consecutive years can cause automatic loss of tax-exempt status. 
